{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## CORRECTION: sweep (windows) is NOT a main-green problem. I misread a non-voting job as a red gate.\n\nI dispatched an agent treating 'sweep (windows) fails on every scheduled run' as a standing main-red\nitem. It is not. test.yml:340-344 gates that job on schedule/workflow_dispatch and sets\ncontinue-on-error: true, with the comment 'Windows is not yet a declared support target, so a genuine\nincompatibility is a visible finding, never a red gate'. gerrit-verify.yaml never references test.yml\nor any Windows job. Runs 33792688019 / 33757757270 / 33725525516 conclude SUCCESS with sweep (windows)\n= failure. I compared job conclusions without checking whether the job could affect the run\nconclusion -- the same category of error as reading vote states without reading failing job lists.\n\nAND THE PREMISE WAS WRONG TWICE: only ONE of the four runs (33824556786) is red at RUN level, and\nsweep (windows) is not why. Its other failing job is the 9a17 e2e crash -- and 'git merge-base\n--is-ancestor 668523feffde 85ef5ad33ada' confirms the fix is NOT an ancestor of that run's head, so\nit predates the fix. 9a17 IS NOT RECURRING POST-FIX on current evidence. That is the single most\nimportant line in this entry.\n\nTHE AGENT STOPPED BEFORE AN UNSAFE FIX, WHICH IS THE RESULT I MOST WANTED. lock.py:72-75 guards the\nfcntl IMPORT but not its USE at :207/:254, so every store write raises AttributeError on Windows --\n377 of 510 failures, and it looks like a two-line fix. It is not. _acquire_mkdir (:325-362) passes\nfcntl_held=True into stale-lock reclamation and its docstring says the flag is load-bearing:\nreclamation is race-safe ONLY because the caller already holds the exclusive fcntl leg, so a live\nowner could not be there. Skip that leg and fcntl_held=True becomes a lie -- rebar would reclaim a\nwrite lock held by a LIVE process, i.e. silent store corruption under concurrency. A real fix needs\nmsvcrt.locking/LockFileEx as an alternative exclusive leg plus a reworked staleness proof.\n\nNEVER PASSED: added by 7024c35790bc on 2026-08-26 and red on every run since. No regression; the\nsweep simply revealed rebar does not run on Windows. 115 distinct files, ~8 independent workstreams.\n\nFiled f850-674c-c89f-4b97 / unsuited-locustal-incatern (p2) with the disposition stated as a FORK\nrather than assumed: (a) adopt Windows as a support target and make this a portability epic with\nlock.py first, or (b) keep it unsupported and stop pretending the job is a signal -- retire it or\nmove it to an explicitly informational workflow. (b) is explicitly not framed as the lesser option;\ngiven the stated non-support position it may be right. What is indefensible is the status quo: a\npermanently-red job that looks like a failure and is documented to be ignorable. It already cost\nreal time -- mine, this cycle.\n\n### TRUE MAIN-GREEN STATE AFTER THE CORRECTION\nOnly ONE genuine blocker remains: scanner-integration, owned by another session, failing on main\nitself. 9a17 is fixed and not recurring. sweep (windows) is informational.\n\n### DECISION MADE AUTONOMOUSLY, no prompting per standing instruction\nAll five open changes (2594, 2601, 2607, 2608, 2609) carry LLM-Review +1 and fail on some mix of\nscanner-integration plus pytest cells whose runs PREDATE the 9a17 fix. Rebasing them now onto current\nmain would clear the pytest half -- but scanner-integration would still fail every one of them, so\nthey would not reach Verified +1 and I would have spent five full CI runs to learn nothing. HOLDING\nthe rebases until the scanner job is fixed, then rebasing all five onto a main carrying both fixes.\nRecorded here so the reasoning is visible rather than looking like neglect."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788500916728882001,"uuid":"d947a1d5-06ad-4bbf-adb5-7e4ef4d32894"}